\subsection{Detection Boundary}
\label{sec:theory}

We model the PermG detection rate as a $T$-dependent step function, fitted jointly to $T{=}10$ ($n{=}17$ $\alpha$ values) and $T{=}20$ ($n{=}5$) data, excluding the synchronous dip at $\alpha \in [0.55, 0.65]$ where structural non-monotonicity invalidates the sigmoid assumption:
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{rate}(\alpha, T) = \frac{0.70}{1 + \exp\bigl(-100\,(\alpha - \alpha_c(T))\bigr)}, \quad R^2 = 0.883.
\label{eq:joint-sigmoid}
\end{equation}
The detection boundary follows a power law in observation length:
\begin{equation}
\alpha_c(T) = 0.193 + 75.68 \cdot T^{-2.41}.
\label{eq:alpha-c}
\end{equation}
The steepness $k{=}100$ indicates a step-like transition from undetectable to ${\sim}70\%$ detection within a narrow contamination band.
At $T{=}10$, $\alpha_c = 0.487$, matching the empirical onset (\S\ref{sec:regime}); at $T{=}20$, $\alpha_c = 0.248$, consistent with the leftward boundary shift observed in extended experiments (\S\ref{sec:t20}).
The underlying power structure decomposes into signal $S(\alpha)$ (magnitude of distributional shift, monotonically increasing) and resolution $R(\alpha)$ (generational gap between canary and downstream responses): $R$ drops at $\alpha{\approx}0.60$ when relaxation time matching compresses both responses into a single generation, producing a synchronous dip despite rising $S$.
This predicts four regimes: \emph{blind} ($S$ below detection floor), \emph{onset} ($S$ crosses threshold), \emph{synchronous dip} ($R \to 0$), and \emph{detection} ($S$ dominates).

